[STEP 1] Recruitment
Group 1 -  Initial Assessment Team (IAT)
 Member 1 (Pediatric Immunologist (Lead)): Specializes in diagnosing and managing immunodeficiency diseases in children, focusing on assessing T and B cell function which is crucial for this case's evaluation.
 Member 2 (Pediatric Infectious Disease Specialist): Provides expertise in managing opportunistic infections and in interpreting the signs and symptoms of infections, including RSV pneumonia and oral candidiasis.
 Member 3 (Pediatric Gastroenterologist): Evaluates gastrointestinal symptoms such as persistent diarrhea, offering insights into potential malabsorption or other GI issues related to immunodeficiency.

Group 2 -  Diagnostic Evidence Team (DET)
 Member 1 (Clinical Laboratory Scientist (Lead)): Oversees laboratory testing and evaluates results related to immune function (e.g., blood cell counts, immunoglobulin levels) to confirm findings of reduced B cells or defective T cell function.
 Member 2 (Geneticist): Assesses the potential for hereditary immunodeficiency syndromes through genetic testing and family history, helping to identify possible X
 Member 3 (Pediatric Hematologist): Focuses on the evaluation of blood cells, including a thorough assessment of lymphocytes and their subsets, which is critical for diagnosing issues related to B or T cell populations.

Group 3 -  Final Review and Decision Team (FRDT)
 Member 1 (Senior Consultant Pediatrician (Lead)): Integrates findings from all assessments, guiding discussions based on overall clinical impressions and alignment with standard treatment protocols for immunodeficiencies.
 Member 2 (Immunology Clinical Nurse Specialist): Supports the team in conducting thorough reviews of the patient's case history and treatment responses, ensuring holistic consideration of the patient's condition and nursing implications.
 Member 3 (Pediatric Oncologist): While not primarily focused on immunodeficiencies, their expertise in lymphocyte biology and treatment plans for abnormal immune responses can provide insight into managing potential malignancies or other sequelae of immunodeficiencies.


